\title{Structural Reproducibility:\\ An Arithmetic Theory of Audited Pipeline Statistics}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{23 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Computational research increasingly relies on \emph{reproducibility audits}: a seeded,
deterministic pipeline is re-executed from stored seeds and the freshly produced table
of statistics is compared, digit for digit, with the recorded one. Agreement is reported
as ``zero drift.'' We argue that this practice conflates two very different notions ---
\emph{executional} reproducibility, a property of two runs, and \emph{structural}
reproducibility, a property of the model --- and we develop an arithmetic theory that
separates them.

Working with a concrete model of a seeded pipeline (the linear congruential map
$x \mapsto (ax+c) \bmod m$ and, more generally, an arbitrary endofunction with a finite
state window), we prove that four families of routinely audited statistics are theorems
rather than measurements, and therefore cannot drift under any re-execution: rebatching
invariance of the terminal state; capacity saturation with a nondecreasing, divergent
deficit column; the synergy decomposition of two periodic channels, with nonnegative
synergy and overlap coefficient in $[0,1]$; and the exactness of the clipped ramp law for
lexicographic grid enumeration.

We then strengthen each into a quantitative statement. We prove an \emph{exact orbit-count
law}: for any deterministic pipeline whose orbit lies in a window of size $m$ there is a
single integer $N \le m$ with $|S_k| = \min(k+1,N)$ for every $k$; finiteness alone forces
the shape. This yields a \emph{deficit slope detector}: consecutive deficits differ by
exactly one bit if and only if the orbit is already exhausted, so the orbit size is
recoverable from the published deficit column alone. We prove a \emph{rebatching
characterisation}: a schedule-executing process on an arbitrary state type is batch
invariant if and only if it is the iterate of its unit step, so batch sensitivity is a
complete certificate of hidden state. We derive the closed form
$S(p,q) = \log_2(\min(p,q)/\gcd(p,q))$ and deduce a \emph{one-bit quantisation gap}:
non-nested integer channels have $S \ge 1$, with equality exactly when
$\min(p,q) = 2\gcd(p,q)$.

The gap has an adversarial consequence. Audited synergy values reported at $+0.1290$ and
$+0.0049$ bits lie in a region the model provably cannot reach. Perfect executional
reproducibility is therefore compatible with a statistic that cannot bear its stated
interpretation --- a failure mode re-execution is structurally unable to detect.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} reproducibility; deterministic pipelines; linear congruential
maps; orbit counting; capacity saturation; synergy decomposition; lcm--gcd
inclusion--exclusion; quantisation gap.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Two meanings of ``reproducible''}

When a computational programme reports that its keystone results ``reproduce exactly under
stored seeds,'' it asserts that a second execution of a deterministic pipeline, initialised
identically, produced an identical table. Call this \emph{executional reproducibility}. It
is falsifiable, cheap to test, and catches real problems: unseeded randomness, dependence
on thread scheduling, accumulated floating-point drift, environment leakage.

It is also an existential statement about a finite sample of runs. It cannot distinguish
between a number that is stable \emph{because the underlying mathematics admits no
alternative} and a number that is stable \emph{because the same computation was performed
twice}. Call the former \emph{structural reproducibility}: a theorem about the model,
invariant not merely across executions but across implementations, languages, hardware,
and reasonable perturbations of the code.

The distinction cuts both ways. A structurally reproducible statistic does not need
auditing, and auditing it spends the audit's credibility on a foregone conclusion. A
statistic that is executionally reproducible may nonetheless be \emph{impossible} under
the model it is said to instantiate; determinism guarantees that a misinterpretation
reproduces perfectly.

\subsection{The audited statistics}

The audit motivating this work reports four kinds of quantity: a terminal state claimed
independent of batching; a capacity curve $I(k)=\log_2|S_k|$ with deficits
$d(k)=k-I(k)$ recorded from $+0.000$ to $+6.372$ with $I(6)=11.5307$; a synergy
decomposition reported as $+0.1290$ and $+0.0049$ bits with overlap $0.9919$; and a
success fraction $P_1 \approx \ramp(q/r^2)$. We model each precisely, prove the
qualitative claim, and push to the exact quantitative statement.

\section{The pipeline model}

\begin{definition}[Congruential step]
Fix $a,c,m \in \mathbb{N}$. The \emph{step map} is
$\sigma_{a,c,m}(x) = (ax+c) \bmod m$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Run]
The \emph{run} of $n$ steps from seed $s$ is $R_0(s)=s$ and $R_{n+1}(s)=R_n(\sigma(s))$,
suppressing parameters when clear. Equivalently $R_n = \sigma^{\,n}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Window confinement]\label{lem:window}
If $m>0$ and $s<m$ then $R_n(s)<m$ for all $n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$; the step case uses $(ax+c)\bmod m < m$ for $m>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Batched execution]
For a schedule $L=[n_1,\dots,n_r]$ put
$E(L,s) = R_{n_r}(\cdots R_{n_2}(R_{n_1}(s))\cdots)$, the left fold of the run operation,
with $E([\,],s)=s$. Write $\Norm{L}=n_1+\cdots+n_r$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Visited set]
$S_k = \{R_i(s) : 0 \le i \le k\}$.
\end{definition}

Immediately $S_0=\{s\}$, so $|S_k|\ge 1$; $S_k \subseteq S_{k+1}$ and
$S_{k+1} = S_k \cup \{R_{k+1}(s)\}$, so $|S_{k+1}| \le |S_k|+1$; and if $m>0$, $s<m$ then
$|S_k| \le m$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:window}.

\section{Keystone R: rebatching invariance and its converse}

\begin{theorem}[Additivity of runs]\label{thm:add}
For all $p,q,s$: $R_{p+q}(s) = R_q(R_p(s))$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $p$, generalising $s$. For $p=0$ both sides are $R_q(s)$. For the step,
$R_{(p+1)+q}(s) = R_{p+q}(\sigma(s)) = R_q(R_p(\sigma(s))) = R_q(R_{p+1}(s))$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Batched execution collapses]\label{thm:batch}
$E(L,s) = R_{\Norm{L}}(s)$ for every schedule $L$ and seed $s$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $L$. For $L=[\,]$ both sides are $s$. For $L = n :: L'$,
$E(n::L',s) = E(L',R_n(s)) = R_{\Norm{L'}}(R_n(s)) = R_{n+\Norm{L'}}(s)$ by the inductive
hypothesis and Theorem~\ref{thm:add}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Zero drift]\label{cor:zerodrift}
If $\Norm{L}=\Norm{L'}$ then $E(L,s)=E(L',s)$ for every seed $s$ and all $a,c,m$.
\end{corollary}

The terminal state is thus a function of the seed and the total step count alone; it cannot
be perturbed by checkpointing, restarting, distributing, or re-chunking the run. No
execution can produce a counterexample, so no audit can supply evidence for it.

\subsection{The converse: batch sensitivity certifies hidden state}

\begin{definition}[Schedule-executing process]
Let $S$ be any type. A map $F:\mathrm{List}\,\mathbb{N} \to S \to S$ is a
\emph{schedule-executing process} if $F([\,],s)=s$ for all $s$ and
$F(n::L,s) = F(L,F([n],s))$ for all $n,L,s$. Write $\phi := F([1],\cdot)$ for its
\emph{unit step}. Batched execution $E$ satisfies both conditions by definition.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Unit schedules iterate]\label{lem:unitsched}
$F(\underbrace{[1,\dots,1]}_{n},s) = \phi^{\,n}(s)$ for all $n,s$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $n$, generalising $s$; the base case is the empty law and the step case is the
cons law followed by the inductive hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rebatching Characterisation]\label{thm:rebatch}
A schedule-executing process $F$ satisfies
\[ \Norm{L}=\Norm{L'} \implies F(L,s)=F(L',s) \quad\text{for all } L,L',s \]
if and only if $F(L,s) = \phi^{\,\Norm{L}}(s)$ for all $L,s$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Both sides depend on $L$ only through $\Norm{L}$.
($\Rightarrow$) The schedule $[1,\dots,1]$ of length $\Norm{L}$ has total $\Norm{L}$, so
batch invariance gives $F(L,s) = F([1,\dots,1],s) = \phi^{\Norm{L}}(s)$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:unitsched}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Batch sensitivity certifies hidden state]
If $\Norm{L}=\Norm{L'}$ and $F(L,s)\ne F(L',s)$ for some seed $s$, then $F$ is not the
iterate of any unit step: no visible step map on $S$ explains the process.
\end{corollary}

The equivalence upgrades a familiar engineering heuristic into a decision procedure: the
absence of hidden state is not merely suggested by batch invariance, it is equivalent to it.

\begin{proposition}
$E(L,s) = \sigma^{\Norm{L}}(s)$, and Corollary~\ref{cor:zerodrift} is an instance of
Theorem~\ref{thm:rebatch}.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$E(L,s)=R_{\Norm{L}}(s)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:batch} and $R_n=\sigma^n$ by induction; the
unit step of $E$ is $E([1],\cdot)=\sigma$.
\end{proof}

\section{Keystone C: capacity and the saturating deficit}

\begin{definition}
$I(k) = \log_2 |S_k|$ bits, and $d(k) = k - I(k)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$I(k) \ge 0$ and $I$ is nondecreasing.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$|S_k|\ge 1$; and $S_k \subseteq S_l$ for $k\le l$, with $\log_2$ monotone on positives.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Saturation ceiling]\label{thm:ceiling}
If $m>0$ and $s<m$ then $I(k) \le \log_2 m$ for all $k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$|S_k| \le m$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:window}; apply $\log_2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[One bit per step]\label{thm:onebit}
$I(k+1) \le I(k)+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
With $A=|S_k|\ge1$ we have $|S_{k+1}| \le A+1 \le 2A$; take $\log_2$ and use
$\log_2(2A)=1+\log_2 A$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The deficit column]
Under $m>0$, $s<m$: $d(0)=0$; $d$ is nondecreasing; and $d(k)\to+\infty$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$|S_0|=1$ gives $I(0)=0$ and $d(0)=0$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:onebit},
$d(k+1)-d(k) = 1-(I(k+1)-I(k)) \ge 0$; chain along $k \le l$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:ceiling}, $d(k) \ge k - \log_2 m \to \infty$.
\end{proof}

The reported profile --- beginning at exactly $+0.000$, never decreasing, climbing without
bound as capacity flattens --- is forced by the model and carries no information about the
particular execution.

\section{The exact orbit-count law and the deficit slope detector}

Let $f:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$, let $s$ be a seed, and write
$\mathcal{O}_k = \{f^{\,i}(s) : 0 \le i \le k\}$.

\begin{lemma}[Stalling is permanent]\label{lem:stall}
If $\mathcal{O}_{k+1}=\mathcal{O}_k$ then $\mathcal{O}_{k+2}=\mathcal{O}_{k+1}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
From the hypothesis, $f^{k+1}(s)=f^{\,i}(s)$ for some $i \le k$. Applying $f$,
$f^{k+2}(s)=f^{\,i+1}(s)$ with $i+1\le k+1$, so $f^{k+2}(s) \in \mathcal{O}_{k+1}$ and
adjoining it changes nothing.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:frozen}
If $\mathcal{O}_{K+1}=\mathcal{O}_K$ then $\mathcal{O}_k = \mathcal{O}_K$ for all $k \ge K$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Strict growth is by one]\label{lem:growone}
If $\mathcal{O}_{k+1}\ne\mathcal{O}_k$ then $|\mathcal{O}_{k+1}| = |\mathcal{O}_k|+1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\mathcal{O}_{k+1} = \mathcal{O}_k \cup \{f^{k+1}(s)\}$ and the new element is not already
present, else the sets would coincide.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Finiteness forces a stall]\label{lem:mustball}
If $f^{\,i}(s) < m$ for all $i$, then $\mathcal{O}_{k+1}=\mathcal{O}_k$ for some $k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Otherwise Lemma~\ref{lem:growone} gives $|\mathcal{O}_k|=k+1$ for all $k$ by induction;
taking $k=m$ contradicts $|\mathcal{O}_m| \le m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact Orbit-Count Law]\label{thm:orbitcount}
Let $f:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ and $s$ satisfy $f^{\,i}(s)<m$ for all $i$. Then there is
an integer $N$ with $1 \le N \le m$ such that
\[ |\mathcal{O}_k| \;=\; \min(k+1,\,N) \qquad \text{for every } k \ge 0. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $K$ be least with $\mathcal{O}_{K+1}=\mathcal{O}_K$, which exists by
Lemma~\ref{lem:mustball}. By minimality and Lemma~\ref{lem:growone},
$|\mathcal{O}_k| = k+1$ for all $k \le K$. Put $N=K+1$; then $N \ge 1$ and
$N = |\mathcal{O}_K| \le m$. For $k \le K$, $\min(k+1,N)=k+1=|\mathcal{O}_k|$; for $k \ge K$,
Corollary~\ref{cor:frozen} gives $|\mathcal{O}_k|=N=\min(k+1,N)$.
\end{proof}

No periodicity, linearity, or algebraic structure is assumed: finiteness alone determines
the entire count sequence up to one integer.

\begin{corollary}
For any $a,c$, any $m>0$ and any $s<m$ there is $N$ with $1 \le N \le m$ and
$|S_k| = \min(k+1,N)$ for all $k$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Deficit Slope Detector]\label{thm:detector}
Suppose $|S_k| = \min(k+1,N)$ for all $k$. Then
\[ d(k+1)-d(k) = 1 \iff N \le k+1, \]
and when $k+2 \le N$ the difference is strictly less than $1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $N \le k+1$ then $|S_k|=|S_{k+1}|=N$, so $I(k+1)=I(k)$ and the difference is $1$. If
$k+2 \le N$ then $|S_k|=k+1$, $|S_{k+1}|=k+2$ and
$d(k+1)-d(k) = 1 - \log_2\frac{k+2}{k+1} < 1$. The cases are exhaustive and exclusive.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Orbit size is observable]\label{cor:observable}
$N$ equals the least $k+1$ for which $d(k+1)-d(k)=1$, and is therefore recoverable from the
published deficit column alone, with no access to the pipeline's internals.
\end{corollary}

This also constrains what a valid deficit column can look like: unit jumps must form a
terminal segment. A column with a unit jump followed later by a sub-unit jump is
inconsistent with any deterministic finite-state pipeline.

\subsection{The full-period rotation and the extremal envelope}

\begin{theorem}[Rotation dynamics]
For $a=c=1$, $m>0$, $s<m$: $R_n(s) = (s+n)\bmod m$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $n$; the base is $s \bmod m = s$ and the step uses $\sigma(s)=(s+1)\bmod m$
together with $((s+1)\bmod m + n)\bmod m = (s+1+n)\bmod m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact rotation orbit count]\label{thm:rotcount}
Under the same hypotheses, $|S_k| = \min(k+1,m)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$S_k$ is the image of $\{0,\dots,k\}$ under $i \mapsto (s+i)\bmod m$. If $k+1 \le m$ the map
is injective there (equal residues force $i \equiv j \pmod m$ with both below $m$), so the
image has $k+1$ elements. If $m \le k+1$ the image is all of $\{0,\dots,m-1\}$: it is
contained there, and any $x<m$ is hit by $i = (x+m-s)\bmod m \le k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The exact curve]
For the rotation pipeline $I(k)=\log_2\min(k+1,m)$ and $d(k)=k-\log_2\min(k+1,m)$: the
entire capacity column is a function of the single parameter $m$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Unit slope after saturation]
If $m \le k+1$ then $d(k+1)-d(k)=1$ exactly.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Strict ramping before saturation]
If $1 \le k$ and $k+2 \le m$ then $d(k) < d(k+1)$ strictly.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
$d(k+1)-d(k)=1-\log_2\frac{k+2}{k+1}$, and $\frac{k+2}{k+1}<2$ for $k\ge1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Capacity envelope]
For every congruential pipeline on modulus $m$ with $s<m$ and every $k$,
$I_{a,c}(k) \le \log_2\min(k+1,m)$, with equality for $a=c=1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$|S_k| \le k+1$ (image of a $(k{+}1)$-element set) and $|S_k| \le m$
(Lemma~\ref{lem:window}), so $|S_k| \le \min(k+1,m)$; apply $\log_2$. Equality is
Theorem~\ref{thm:rotcount}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Deficit envelope]
$d_{1,1}(k) \le d_{a,c}(k)$ for all $a,c$: the full-period rotation has the smallest
possible deficit at every time.
\end{corollary}

\section{Keystone S: the synergy calculus of two periodic channels}

\begin{definition}
For periods $p,q \ge 1$, observing both channels for $k$ ticks yields
$J_k(p,q) = \{(i \bmod p,\ i \bmod q) : 0 \le i < k\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Joint orbit size]\label{thm:joint}
$|J_{\lcm(p,q)}(p,q)| = \lcm(p,q)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
It suffices that $i \mapsto (i\bmod p, i \bmod q)$ is injective on
$\{0,\dots,\lcm(p,q)-1\}$. If $i \le j$ in that range share an image then $p \mid j-i$ and
$q \mid j-i$, so $\lcm(p,q) \mid j-i$; but $0 \le j-i < \lcm(p,q)$, forcing $j=i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
$I(n) = \log_2 n$ for $n \ge 1$ (channel capacity in bits).
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Inclusion--exclusion for capacity]\label{thm:incexc}
For $p,q\ge1$: $I(\lcm(p,q)) + I(\gcd(p,q)) = I(p)+I(q)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $\log_2$ of $\gcd(p,q)\lcm(p,q)=pq$ and split each product logarithm; all arguments
are positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
$S(p,q) = I(\lcm(p,q)) - \max(I(p),I(q))$ and
$\Omega(p,q) = I(\gcd(p,q))/\min(I(p),I(q))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Synergy is nonnegative]
$S(p,q) \ge 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$p,q$ both divide, hence are at most, $\lcm(p,q)$; $\log_2$ is monotone.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Vanishing synergy is nesting]
$S(p,q)=0$ iff $p \mid q$ or $q \mid p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Strict monotonicity of $\log_2$ gives $\max(I(p),I(q))=I(\max(p,q))$, so $S=0$ iff
$\lcm(p,q)=\max(p,q)$. The lcm always dominates the max, and equality holds iff the max is
a common multiple, i.e.\ iff the min divides the max.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Overlap lies in $[0,1]$]
For $p,q \ge 2$: $0 \le \Omega(p,q) \le 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\gcd \ge 1$ gives $I(\gcd)\ge0$, and $p,q\ge2$ give $I(p),I(q)>0$, so $\Omega \ge 0$. And
$\gcd(p,q)$ is at most both $p$ and $q$, so $I(\gcd) \le \min(I(p),I(q))$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Order-theoretic twin]\label{lem:twin}
$I(\max(p,q)) + I(\min(p,q)) = I(p)+I(q)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\max(p,q)\min(p,q) = pq$; take logarithms.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Closed form for synergy]\label{thm:closed}
For $p,q \ge 1$:
\[ S(p,q) \;=\; I(\min(p,q)) - I(\gcd(p,q)) \;=\; \log_2\frac{\min(p,q)}{\gcd(p,q)} . \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Using $\max(I(p),I(q)) = I(\max(p,q))$ and subtracting Lemma~\ref{lem:twin} from
Theorem~\ref{thm:incexc},
$S = I(\lcm)-I(\max) = (I(p)+I(q)-I(\gcd)) - (I(p)+I(q)-I(\min)) = I(\min)-I(\gcd)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[One-bit quantisation gap]\label{thm:gap}
If $p,q \ge 1$ and neither divides the other, then $S(p,q) \ge 1$. Hence always
$S(p,q) \in \{0\} \cup [1,\infty)$: no synergy strictly between zero and one bit is
attainable.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $g=\gcd(p,q)$ and $\mu=\min(p,q)$, so $g \mid \mu$, say $\mu = tg$. If $t=1$ then
$g=\mu$, so $\mu$ divides both $p$ and $q$ --- the nested case, excluded; $t=0$ would give
$\mu=0$. Hence $t \ge 2$, so $\mu \ge 2g$ and $S = \log_2(\mu/g) \ge 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[When the gap is attained]
$S(p,q)=1$ if and only if $\min(p,q) = 2\gcd(p,q)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$S=1$ iff $I(\min) = 1 + I(\gcd) = I(2\gcd)$, and $\log_2$ is strictly monotone.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Overlap in closed form, and a partition of unity]
$\Omega(p,q) = I(\gcd(p,q))/I(\min(p,q))$ for $p,q\ge1$, and for $p,q \ge 2$
\[ \Omega(p,q) + \frac{S(p,q)}{I(\min(p,q))} = 1 . \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Strict monotonicity of $\log_2$ gives $\min(I(p),I(q)) = I(\min(p,q))$. For the second
claim, $I(\min)>0$ when $\min \ge 2$, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:closed} the numerator sums to
$I(\gcd) + (I(\min)-I(\gcd)) = I(\min)$.
\end{proof}

The two audited statistics are therefore complementary shares of the weaker channel's
capacity: $\Omega$ is the redundant fraction and $S/I(\min)$ the novel fraction. Reporting
both is reporting one number twice.

\section{Keystone P: the ramp law is exact}

\begin{definition}
$\ramp(x) = \max(0,\min(1,x))$, the clamp of $x$ to $[0,1]$; it is monotone.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
Enumerate the cells of the $r\times r$ grid in reading order, so $(i,j)$ with
$0 \le i,j < r$ has index $ir+j$. For a budget $q \ge 0$ set
$C(q,r) = |\{(i,j) \in [0,r)^2 : ir+j < q\}|$ and $P_1(q,r) = C(q,r)/r^2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact cell count]\label{thm:cellcount}
$C(q,r) = \min(q, r^2)$ for all $q \ge 0$, $r \ge 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $r=0$ both sides vanish. For $r \ge 1$, the maps $(i,j)\mapsto ir+j$ and
$n \mapsto (n \operatorname{div} r,\ n \bmod r)$ are mutually inverse bijections between the
counted set and $\{0,\dots,\min(q,r^2)-1\}$. Forward: $i,j<r$ and $ir+j<q$ give also
$ir+j < (i+1)r \le r^2$. Backward: $n < \min(q,r^2)$ gives $n\operatorname{div} r<r$,
$n \bmod r < r$ and $(n\operatorname{div}r)r + (n\bmod r) = n < q$. Mutual inversion is
division with remainder.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The ramp law, exactly]
For $r \ge 1$ and all $q \ge 0$: $P_1(q,r) = \ramp\!\left(q/r^2\right)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:cellcount}, $P_1 = \min(q,r^2)/r^2$. If $q \le r^2$ this is
$q/r^2 \in [0,1]$, fixed by the clamp; if $q \ge r^2$ it is $1$ and $q/r^2 \ge 1$, so the
clamp returns $1$.
\end{proof}

The reported approximation is therefore an identity: the ``clipping'' is not a fitted
nonlinearity but the two branches of $\min(q,r^2)$, and the linear regime has slope exactly
$1/r^2$.

\section{The audit bundle}

\begin{theorem}[Structural audit bundle]
Let $m>0$, $s<m$, $p,q \ge 2$, $r \ge 1$, and let $L,L'$ be schedules with
$\Norm{L}=\Norm{L'}$. Then all of the following are theorems of the model:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $E(L,s)=E(L',s)$: the recorded terminal state does not depend on the batch schedule;
\item $d(0)=0$ and $d(k) \le d(l)$ whenever $k \le l$;
\item $d(k)\to\infty$: the capacity curve saturates;
\item $S(p,q)\ge0$ and $\Omega(p,q)\in[0,1]$;
\item $P_1(n,r) = \ramp(n/r^2)$ for every $n$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

None can fail under any execution, on any hardware, in any language, for any seed.
Verifying them by re-execution therefore provides no evidence beyond the correctness of the
implementation. Beyond these we have the strictly quantitative results: the count column of
any deterministic finite-state pipeline is $\min(k+1,N)$ for a single orbit size $N$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:orbitcount}); $N$ is readable off the deficit column
(Corollary~\ref{cor:observable}); batch invariance is \emph{equivalent} to iterated unit
stepping (Theorem~\ref{thm:rebatch}); and synergy is quantised with a full-bit gap above
zero (Theorem~\ref{thm:gap}).

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{The audit's blind spot}

Items (i)--(v) are invariant under re-execution by mathematical necessity. Confirming them
empirically tests the implementation against the model, but cannot fail informatively: a
violation indicates a coding error, never a scientific finding. Conversely, determinism
guarantees that a \emph{misinterpreted} statistic reproduces just as faithfully as a correct
one, so executional reproducibility is silent about semantic validity.

\subsection{An adversarial finding: two synergy values in the forbidden zone}

The audited synergy table reports $+0.1290$ bits for one channel pair and $+0.0049$ bits for
another, both reproducing exactly, alongside an overlap coefficient of $0.9919$.

By Theorem~\ref{thm:gap}, for two channels with exact integer periods the synergy
$S(p,q)=\log_2(\min(p,q)/\gcd(p,q))$ is the base-$2$ logarithm of a positive integer, hence
lies in $\{0,\,1,\,\log_2 3 \approx 1.585,\,2,\dots\} \subseteq \{0\}\cup[1,\infty)$.
Neither $0.1290$ nor $0.0049$ is in that set; both lie in the open gap $(0,1)$ the model
provably cannot reach.

Carefully stated: it does \emph{not} follow that the computation is wrong or that the audit
failed --- the numbers may be exactly what the code computes and may reproduce forever. It
\emph{does} follow that the reported quantity is not the joint-versus-marginal capacity of
two exactly periodic integer channels. Candidate reconciliations, each of which changes what
the number means:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item \textbf{Truncated observation.} If the joint channel is observed for $k < \lcm(p,q)$
ticks, the count is $\min(k,\lcm(p,q))$ and the quantisation argument lapses; the value is a
window artifact.
\item \textbf{Approximate periodicity.} For quasi-periodic or noisy channels the arithmetic
of $\gcd$ and $\lcm$ is unavailable --- the gap dissolves, but so does the
inclusion--exclusion identity motivating the statistic.
\item \textbf{Averaging.} An average of per-pair synergies can land in $(0,1)$ while every
summand is $0$ or $\ge 1$; indeed $0.0049$ is consistent with one unit-synergy pair among
roughly $200$ nested pairs. The value then measures prevalence of non-nesting.
\item \textbf{Smoothed counts.} Logarithms of shrunk or smoothed counts are not logarithms
of orbit sizes and inherit none of the arithmetic.
\end{enumerate}
The theorem cannot say which is operative, but it establishes that one of them must be ---
a conclusion no amount of re-execution could reach.

\subsection{A second admissibility check: the capacity column}

The same discipline applies to the reported capacity numbers. In the single-orbit model the
two columns are rigidly linked: since $|S_k| \le k+1$ we have $I(k) \le \log_2(k+1)$, hence
$d(k)=k-I(k) \ge 0$ with $d(0)=0$. A reported value $I(6)=11.5307$ bits is therefore
impossible for the number of distinct states seen by a single pipeline at time $6$, which
cannot exceed $7$, giving $I(6) \le \log_2 7 \approx 2.807$; the corresponding deficit
$6-11.5307 < 0$ would violate nonnegativity.

Again the conclusion is not that a computation is wrong, but that the recorded capacity and
the recorded deficit column cannot both be the single-orbit quantities defined above: at
least one measures something else --- plausibly the joint capacity of a battery of several
channels, with the deficit taken relative to the sum of the marginal capacities rather than
relative to the clock. That is a sensible statistic obeying a different set of constraints,
and the ones proved here do not transfer to it unexamined. A reported pair $(I(k),d(k))$
violating $0 \le d(k)$ or $I(k) \le \log_2(k+1)$ signals that two different notions are
sharing a column heading.

\subsection{A positive methodology}

\textbf{Tier 1 --- prove the invariants.} Identify which reported quantities are theorems;
report them as theorems and spend no audit budget on them.
\textbf{Tier 2 --- publish admissibility constraints.} For quantities that are not
determined, derive what the model forbids: unit jumps in the deficit column must form a
terminal segment; synergy must avoid $(0,1)$; overlap plus synergy share must equal one.
These checks cost one line of arithmetic and catch misinterpretation, not just
nondeterminism.
\textbf{Tier 3 --- audit the residue.} Whatever genuinely depends on execution --- timings,
stochastic estimates, floating-point tails --- is the proper subject of re-execution.

\subsection{Batch sensitivity as a diagnostic}

Theorem~\ref{thm:rebatch} turns folk practice into a decision procedure: running a pipeline
under two schedules of equal total length and comparing outputs \emph{decides} whether the
process is an iterated visible step. Since hidden state (accumulators, caches, reduction
orders, lazily initialised buffers, thread-local randomness) is the most common source of
practical non-reproducibility, and the test requires no instrumentation, this is a cheap and
complete diagnostic.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item \textbf{Orbit size as a published statistic.} Since $N$ determines the entire capacity
column and is observable from the deficits, the honest summary of a capacity experiment is a
single integer. What then is the informative content of publishing the column?
\item \textbf{Beyond a single orbit.} Averaging over seeds gives
$\mathbb{E}_s[\min(k+1,N_s)]$, whose shape depends on the distribution of orbit sizes. For
random maps this meets the classical birthday/rho-length theory; for congruential maps the
orbit-size spectrum is number theoretic. A structural theory of the averaged deficit column
would extend these results to the ensemble setting audits actually use.
\item \textbf{Multi-channel synergy.} For $k$ channels the joint configuration count is
$\lcm(p_1,\dots,p_k)$. Does a quantisation gap survive? The natural conjecture is that any
nontrivial higher-order interaction again costs at least one bit, with the gap governed by
the divisor lattice.
\item \textbf{Approximate periodicity.} Replacing exact periods by approximate ones should
replace the sharp gap by a quantitative bound: how much aperiodicity is needed to realise a
synergy of $\varepsilon$ bits? Such a stability theorem would turn the adversarial finding
into a measurement.
\item \textbf{Rebatching for stateful and stochastic processes.} The corresponding statement
for processes with a declared random tape, or floating-point arithmetic with nonassociative
addition, would give a principled account of when ``same seed, different chunking'' may
legitimately differ.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

We have replaced an empirical reproducibility claim with an arithmetic one. Four families of
audited statistics --- batch-invariant terminal states, the monotone deficit column, the
nonnegative synergy with overlap in $[0,1]$, and the clipped ramp law --- are theorems of the
model and cannot drift. Two sharper families go further: the capacity column of any
deterministic finite-state pipeline is determined by a single integer, the orbit size, which
is readable off the published deficits; and the synergy of two periodic integer channels is
quantised, with closed form $\log_2(\min/\gcd)$ and values in $\{0\}\cup[1,\infty)$.

The last has teeth. Two audited synergy values that reproduce perfectly lie in a region the
model cannot reach, so they cannot bear their stated interpretation. An audit is
constitutionally unable to notice this, because a deterministic pipeline reproduces its own
misinterpretations exactly. Proving what a statistic \emph{can} be is a different and, here,
a strictly more informative act than checking that it came out the same way twice.

